 :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169081} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169082}] :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169077}] :question/math-academy-id "q-159507" :question/problem "The scores on the final calculus test in states X and Y are known to be normally distributed with standard deviations of $σ_{x} = 5$ points and $σ_{y} = 4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean $μ_{x}$ of scores in state X is smaller than the population mean of scores in state Y. They decide to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe scientists sampled $35$ students from state X and $23$ students from state Y, finding that their mean scores are $85$ points and $87$ points, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-2 ± 2.105$.\n2. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that $μ_{x} < μ_{y}$.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169084} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169085} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/va